<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Keep build output in exactly one place per configuration.

      By default MSBuild inserts both the platform and the runtime identifier into the output
      path, so the same source produced three different folders depending on how you invoked the
      build:

        dotnet build                        -> bin\Debug\net10.0-windows\win-x64\
        dotnet build -p:Platform=x64        -> bin\x64\Debug\net10.0-windows\win-x64\
        Visual Studio (the .sln pins x64)   -> bin\x64\Debug\net10.0-windows\win-x64\

      Three stale copies of the app is confusing on its own, but the real hazard is running the
      wrong one and thinking a change didn't take. Neither segment earns its keep here: both
      projects hardcode x64 and win-x64, so there is only ever one platform and one RID to tell
      apart. Turning both off gives a single predictable path:

        bin\<Configuration>\net10.0-windows\           (build)
        bin\<Configuration>\net10.0-windows\publish\   (publish)

      If this project ever targets a second platform or RID, remove these and the segments come
      back automatically.
    -->
    <AppendPlatformToOutputPath>false</AppendPlatformToOutputPath>
    <AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath>false</AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
